Amazon S3 Storage Overview
Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3) is a scalable object storage service. It allows users to store and retrieve any amount of data on the web. S3 boasts features such as versioning, encryption, and lifecycle policies that could be useful in our project regarding user receipts and extracting text from images via Amazon Textract as they integrate seamlessly. 
Using S3 to Store User Images
S3 can be configured to store images uploaded by users in a structured manner, ensuring security, scalability, and efficient retrieval. The service allows for the organization of data into buckets, and within buckets, objects (files) can be arranged hierarchically using prefixes that resemble folder structures.

Our main goals and questions for S3 are as follows:

Is it feasible to store all user images indefinitely? We need to analyze storage space, cost, projected user count and how the storage might look 10 years from now. 

Does S3 offer a way to automatically delete images after a set time period? If indefinite storage proves unrealistic, does S3 offer a way for images/files to automatically delete themselves? This would allow us to store some of a user's recent history for their convenience while not incurring additional storage costs.

Does storing an image prior to scanning with Amazon Textract pose any advantage? (as opposed to just scanning and deleting) Would this possibly add the ability to attempt a scan for text again without the user needing to resubmit an image, or the ability to offer the users recent history and uploads? Possibly a faster extraction process when referenced from S3 storage?

